<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Manteño-Huancavilca</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1535 CE (approx.) · capital Jocay</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Seafaring chiefdoms of the Ecuadorian coast whose balsa rafts carried spondylus shell and trade goods along the Pacific from Mexico to Peru.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Manteño and Huancavilca peoples developed from earlier coastal traditions in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manabi-province">Manabí</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayas-province">Guayas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-elena-province">Santa Elena</a> from around 800. The Manteño occupied the central coast, with Jocay, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manta-ecuador">Manta</a>, as a main port, while the Huancavilca lived to the south around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guayaquil">Gulf of Guayaquil</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The coastal chiefdoms formed a league of lordships under several chiefs. They were skilled sailors: in 1526 the Spanish pilot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-ruiz">Bartolomé Ruiz</a> met a large sailing balsa raft loaded with trade goods off the coast. Spondylus shell, prized in Andean ritual, was a key export. The Manteño carved U-shaped stone seats and stelae, and built settlements with stone foundations on hills such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-de-hojas-jaboncillo">Cerro de Hojas-Jaboncillo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The coastal peoples largely avoided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule. Spanish conquest after 1534, followed by epidemics, devastated the population, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portoviejo">Portoviejo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> were founded in their lands. Descendant communities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-elena-province">Santa Elena</a> peninsula have revived Manteño and Huancavilca identity.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 CE – 1535 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:14.63%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1535 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jocay</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Manteño, Manteño confederation, Huancavilca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1168&amp;focus=manteno-huancavilca" aria-label="Manteño-Huancavilca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2509 2217 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Manteño-Huancavilca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2829 2473h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2829 2473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ecuador. <a href="/atlas?year=1168&amp;focus=manteno-huancavilca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

